## Key Design Decisions

**Why hybrid retrieval?** Dense embeddings capture semantics ("linker error" → linker code) but miss exact identifiers. BM25 matches exact symbol names but misses semantic context. Combining both handles each failure mode.

**Why source path injection?** Many compiler logs contain the exact file path. Rather than hoping retrieval surfaces it, we directly fetch chunks from that file via ChromaDB metadata filtering at score 0.95. This was the single biggest recall improvement (Recall@5: 0.32 → 0.95).

**Why file-level fallback chunks?** Tree-sitter fails to extract functions from ~16% of C++ files (macro-heavy files, very short implementations). Without fallback chunks these files are unretrievable. A `__file__` chunk for zero-function files ensures every file is in the index.

**Why keep LLM separate from retrieval?** Retrieval is deterministic and measurable. LLM is probabilistic and hard to evaluate. Keeping them separate means retrieval quality can be improved and measured independently with Recall@K and MRR.

**Why GraphCodeBERT for code, mpnet for logs?** GraphCodeBERT is trained on code and understands structure. mpnet is the best general semantic model for natural language. They operate in different embedding spaces but cosine similarity still captures meaningful cross-modal relationships — validated by evaluation results.

